From: Philippe Simons <simons.philippe@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] drm/msm/dpu: SM8250 VIG IGC and 3D LUT colour pipelines

Extend the plane colour pipeline (1D LUT -> 3D LUT) to SM8250. Its VIG
pipes carry IGC v6 and gamut v6.0, loaded only through LUTDMA, which on
DPU 6.0 is engine v1.2 (downstream sde: reg_dmav1_setup_vig_igcv6 and
reg_dmav1_setup_vig_gamutv5, read off the stock Pocket Max DT:
qcom,sde-vig-igc/gamut <0x1d00 0x60000>, qcom,sde-reg-dma-version 0x10002).

- Engine v1.2: the v2 register layout (decode select, queue 0 at
  0x14 + 0x10 * CTL, intr status/clear, the CTL 0xd4 software trigger and
  the queue 0 done bits are identical, init_v12 changes no offset) without
  the sideband queue and without the LUT bus, and with the v1 error clear
  mask. The window is 0x214 bytes. Sideband kick-offs are refused.
- REG_BLK_WRITE_MULTIPLE (BIT(30) | BIT(28)), a block write that walks a
  window of registers and wraps, for engines without a LUT bus.
- Gamut v6.0 is written through its registers: per table, TABLE_SEL
  (BIT(12 + table), BIT(11) for the second bank), then {lower, upper}
  pairs through a decrementing two-register block write from LOWER (0xc).
  The 17^3 nodes rotate through the four 1229-entry tables, red fastest.
- The IGC is the same block as on SM8550 and is unchanged.
- An SM8250-only VIG sub-block with the LUTDMA window at 0x200; the shared
  dpu_vig_sblk_qseed3_3_0 is left alone.
- The offline rotator reserves its own pipe and set no LUT requirement --
  nor initialised its requirements -- so a rotated plane with a colour
  pipeline could land on a DMA pipe and skip its transform. It now gets
  the plane's LUT requirement.

The gamut data format is not in downstream, which copies a userspace
table through; it was measured on the Pocket Max through gamescope's
3D LUT override: 10 bits per component, LSB aligned, the upper port
carrying red and the lower port blue in bits 9:0 and green in 25:16.
Solid colours settled the ports, an identity table the node order, and
gradients the width -- a 12-bit value wraps in the 10-bit field, which a
full-scale colour cannot show.
---
